 family which is  present on only one member  so here we know this is very likely an  artifact and we would filter it out  because if it were a true  variant it would be present of course  on the original template molecule in our  input dna  and it should be present in all the  family members because all these family  members with the red  umi are copies of that  specific molecule so here we know this  is an artifact while the  the light blue star we can see they are  present in all family members  in a family and therefore it's very  likely to be a true variant  so this really enhances sensitivity and  specificity and allows us and yet to get  to a limit of detection of  around between 0.1 and 0.5 percent  allelic frequency  so if you now go back to this workflow  what we're hoping now is that with this  technology  we do not have to perform laser capture  micro dissection anymore for the load  cellularity samples but instead actually  we can now for all of the samples that  we have we can  do manual collection we can scrape off  all the cells  we get a lot of material we use the  precision assay  on the genexo sequencer to also get the  shortest turnaround time  and to get these results as quickly as  possible  so we started to test this and what i  show you here  are three different samples you see here  three  cytology smears two of which have an  estimated tumor cell content of ten  percent and one  uh at five percent  from all of these samples we had we  previously had additional smears  which we had later microdissected  uh and then sequenced so you can see in  the table below  for all three samples we had identified  different mutations so we have an egfr  l858r and egfr exo19 deletion as well as  the keras g12c  um in the in the  in in the column lcm af you can see the  allylic frequency of these  variants as they were detected when we  enrich tumor cells with laser capture  microtransaction and on the far right  the last column you can see that  all three 